{"name":"Uniform Green","hex":"#4c4623","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"uniform","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Uniform of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unvarying; all the same.","Consistent; conforming to one standard."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/uniform"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Uniform","blocks":[{"text":"A uniform is a variety of costume worn by members of an organization while usually participating in that organization's activity. Modern uniforms are most often worn by armed forces and paramilitary organizations such as police, emergency services, security guards, in some workplaces and schools, and by inmates in prisons. In some countries, some other officials also wear uniforms in their duties; such is the case of the Commissioned Corps of the United States Public Health Service or the French prefects. For some organizations, such as police, it may be illegal for non-members to wear the uniform."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/The_Royal_Marines_from_1664_to_1896.jpg/330px-The_Royal_Marines_from_1664_to_1896.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniform","wikidata":"Q7434"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Cadetes_da_Turma_Sesquicenten%C3%A1rio_da_Batalha_do_Tuiuti_%289597134805%29.jpg/500px-Cadetes_da_Turma_Sesquicenten%C3%A1rio_da_Batalha_do_Tuiuti_%289597134805%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Cadetes_da_Turma_Sesquicenten%C3%A1rio_da_Batalha_do_Tuiuti_%289597134805%29.jpg/1280px-Cadetes_da_Turma_Sesquicenten%C3%A1rio_da_Batalha_do_Tuiuti_%289597134805%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Cadetes_da_Turma_Sesquicenten%C3%A1rio_da_Batalha_do_Tuiuti_%289597134805%29.jpg/500px-Cadetes_da_Turma_Sesquicenten%C3%A1rio_da_Batalha_do_Tuiuti_%289597134805%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Brazilian cadets in 2013 wearing full dress uniform","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACadetes_d